<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Asthma with persistent airflow limitation, type 2 phenotype (J45.40)</content>. Well controlled (Asthma Control Test 21; reliever about once a week; no night symptoms) on budesonide-formoterol with tiotropium. One yellow-zone flare with a cold, managed correctly by his action plan without oral corticosteroids. Too soon after a flare, and with persistent airflow limitation, to step down; reconsider after three months of good control. [S]<list><item>Continue budesonide-formoterol two inhalations twice daily plus as needed and tiotropium two inhalations daily.</item><item>Action plan reviewed; unchanged.</item><item>After any future flare, the clinic books a review within one week.</item><item>Recombinant zoster vaccine dose 1 today; dose 2 in two to six months.</item><item>Asthma review in three months; spirometry within a year of the last.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection with a yellow-zone flare (J06.9)</content>. Resolved. [S]<list><item>No further action.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Exposure to air pollution and indoor solid-fuel smoke (Z77.110)</content>. Exposure plan maintained; heating season starting. [S]<list><item>Keep the open fires out of use through the winter; replace the HEPA filters as the maker advises.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Asthma Control Test</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>21: well controlled</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Peak expiratory flow</td><td>At each visit after a flare, and at home with symptoms</td><td>Completed today</td><td>430 L/min in clinic, 98 percent of his best</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Review within one week after a flare</td><td>Within one week of any exacerbation</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Missed: the one-week review planned at the sick visit was not booked because no slot was free, and no telephone check was arranged. No harm resulted: his diary shows the peak flow back above 400 L/min by the fifth day without prednisolone. Corrective action: the clinic now books a review within one week, by telephone if no slot is free, whenever he calls or is seen for a flare, and this is written into his care plan</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Review 1 to 3 months after a controller change, with an Asthma Control Test</td><td>1 to 3 months after starting or changing controller treatment</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Partly missed: the twelve-week plan set the full review with an Asthma Control Test at four months, beyond the protocol&apos;s 1 to 3 months. The preventive visit at 13 weeks reviewed his symptoms, reliever use, and side effects, but no Asthma Control Test was recorded until today. No harm: he was well controlled at that visit and today. Corrective action: an Asthma Control Test is recorded at any visit where asthma is reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Inhaler technique with his own devices</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Both inhalers used correctly</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence and reliever use</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No missed doses since the flare; reliever about once a week</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Tiotropium side effects (dry mouth, urinary retention, angle-closure glaucoma symptoms)</td><td>After starting and at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Exacerbation history</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One yellow-zone flare with a cold; no oral corticosteroids, emergency visits, or admissions</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Trigger and exposure review: indoor solid-fuel smoke, outdoor air pollution and air quality alerts, tobacco and vaping, occupational exposures, allergens</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Open fires out of use; HEPA air cleaners running; air quality index checked before city trips; no new exposures</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Smoking and vaping status</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Never smoker; no vaping; no secondhand smoke</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Written asthma action plan</td><td>Every visit and after every flare</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed after the flare; he used it correctly; unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Step-up and step-down assessment</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Well controlled at step 5 (add-on tiotropium); no step-down so soon after a flare; reconsider after three months of good control</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Spirometry</td><td>Every 1 to 2 years once stable</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Last done four months ago; next within a year</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>FeNO</td><td>To monitor inhaled-corticosteroid response when needed</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Low on treatment four months ago; control is good</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Comorbidity surveillance</td><td>At diagnosis and at least yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Screened at diagnosis; PHQ-2 0 at the preventive visit</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Vaccination status</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Influenza, COVID-19, and RSV vaccines given five weeks ago; zoster dose 1 today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Biologic candidacy</td><td>When asthma is severe and uncontrolled despite optimized step 4 to 5 treatment with confirmed adherence and technique</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No exacerbations needing oral corticosteroids and not uncontrolled on optimized treatment</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Cause of the cough and breathlessness established by the second visit. Outcome by 1692-03-26: Asthma with persistent airflow limitation confirmed by spirometry, FeNO, eosinophils, DLCO, and CT; tuberculosis, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, and heart failure excluded [S]</item><item>Asthma well controlled (Asthma Control Test 20 or more) with no flare needing oral corticosteroids. In progress; target 1693-03-31 [S]</item><item>No indoor solid-fuel smoke and less outdoor pollution exposure. In progress; target 1693-03-31 [S]</item><item>Six-minute walk distance improved by at least 30 m after pulmonary rehabilitation. Outcome by 1692-06-18: 430 m before and 505 m after an eight-week program [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Recombinant zoster vaccine, dose 1 of 2 [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, asthma and COPD monographs (2026); Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update (2025); 2020 Focused Updates to the Asthma Management Guidelines: A Report from the National Asthma Education and Prevention Program Coordinating Committee Expert Panel Working Group (2020); Stanojevic S, et al. ERS/ATS technical standard on interpretive strategies for routine lung function tests. Eur Respir J 2022;60(1):2101499 (PMID 34949706) (2022); WHO global air quality guidelines: particulate matter (PM2.5 and PM10), ozone, nitrogen dioxide, sulfur dioxide and carbon monoxide (2021); Carlsten C, et al. Personal strategies to minimise effects of air pollution on respiratory health: advice for providers, patients and the public. Eur Respir J 2020;55(6):1902056 (PMID 32241830) (2020); AQI Basics (2026); Air Cleaners and Air Filters in the Home (2026); Adult Immunization Schedule by Age (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Asthma review in three months; zoster dose 2 in two to six months; spirometry within a year of the last. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
